    "blocks": [
      {"id": "title", "type": "markdown", "body": "# Vertical ARA Long-Chain Dyadic Bubble Test"},
      {
        "id": "summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "zenodo-dyadic-bubble-trajectories",
        "body": "## Executive Summary\n\n**The long chain reveals a genuine scale-dependent temporal-order effect, but not a stable Phi-specific same-lineage handover.** Evaluation trajectories moved closer to Phi as the dyadic window expanded, and their true ordering beat a within-root step permutation. The effect did not beat the broken-lineage control, weakened in strict holdout, and did not select the same fixed landmark across splits. The predeclared Phi-specific claim is therefore not supported."
      },
      {"id": "metrics", "type": "metric-strip", "cardIds": ["card-roots", "card-eval-change", "card-free-target"]},
      {
        "id": "question",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "zenodo-dyadic-bubble-trajectories",
        "body": "## Question and measurement\n\nThe test followed one tracked bubble through 33 consecutive positions. At each dyadic level, two adjacent children closed one parent: 1+1 frames, 2+2, 4+4, 8+8 and 16+16. The ratio between the two child displacement magnitudes was measured directly. Phi was a frozen candidate target, not an ingredient used to construct the ratio."
      },
      {
        "id": "free-target-intro",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "zenodo-dyadic-bubble-trajectories",
        "body": "## The long chain does not settle on one universal coordinate\n\nA calibration-only free target and the same descriptive target in later splits move as the temporal scale changes. Evaluation ends at 1.374419, while holdout ends at 1.638332. The long chain makes the scale dependence clearer, but the split difference rules out a stable universal Phi placement in this representation."
      },
      {"id": "free-target-chart", "type": "chart", "chartId": "chart-free-targets"},
      {
        "id": "controls-intro",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "zenodo-dyadic-bubble-trajectories",
        "body": "## Temporal order matters, but same identity is not isolated\n\nEvaluation mean Phi loss decreased by 0.166028 from level 0 to level 4, with a whole-video 95% interval of [-0.230093, -0.116381]. True order improved more than the within-root permutation. Broken lineages, however, improved at least as much; real-minus-broken endpoint change was +0.038025. This points to a broad video or flow-scale structure rather than a uniquely preserved same-bubble lineage handover."
      },
      {"id": "controls-chart", "type": "chart", "chartId": "chart-evaluation-controls"},
      {
        "id": "targets-intro",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "zenodo-dyadic-bubble-trajectories",
        "body": "## Fixed-target identity changes across splits\n\nAt the coarsest evaluation level, sqrt(2) had the lowest mean loss and Phi was significantly worse than 1.5. In strict holdout, Phi had the lowest point estimate. A physical universal target should not require choosing whichever split is favourable, so the target-placement gate fails."
      },
      {"id": "targets-chart", "type": "chart", "chartId": "chart-coarsest-targets"},
      {"id": "targets-table", "type": "table", "tableId": "table-coarsest-targets"},
      {
        "id": "ara-interpretation",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## ARA interpretation\n\nThe productive part of Dylan's intuition survives: moving farther along the same recorded chain exposes a more coherent coarse-scale relation than a single local step. What fails is the stronger identification of that relation with one invariant Phi coordinate. In ARA language, the chain contains scale-dependent child/parent structure, but this dataset does not show a universal Vertical-ARA Phi pillar."
      },
      {
        "id": "limitations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Measurement boundary\n\nThe observations are 2D tracker-assigned centroids sampled at 50 fps, not the full bubble surface or velocity field. Only 40 strict-holdout roots were available, no 64-frame dyadic child level was feasible, and one evaluation chain was excluded because its registered child displacement was below resolution. The negative result applies to this trajectory coordinate, not to every possible physical realization of Vertical ARA."
      },
      {
        "id": "validation",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "zenodo-dyadic-bubble-trajectories",
        "body": "## Validation and reproducibility\n\nAn independent script recomputed every fixed-target loss, free target, endpoint change, slope, coarsest paired comparison and three raw-source trajectory spot checks. All checks passed with zero numerical discrepancy. Whole-tree time reversal preserved the ratio multiset exactly. The frozen protocol, scripts, detailed result and retained CSV/JSON outputs live together in analysis/vertical_ara_bubbles."
      }
    ]
  },
